s","a":["Kimberly Matheson","Barbara Cole"],"d":"2004-05-12","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2004.04.002","pdf":"","k":["Psychology","Coping (psychology)","Stressor","Optimism","Social psychology","Psychosocial","Developmental psychology","Social identity theory"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0022-1031(04)00041-1","t":"The influence of abstract and concrete mindsets on anticipating and guiding others' self-regulatory efforts*1","a":["André Luís Policani Freitas"],"d":"2004-05-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0022-1031(04)00041-1","pdf":"","k":["Mindset","Psychology","Regulatory focus theory","Salience (neuroscience)","Anticipation (artificial intelligence)","Social psychology","Preference","Interpersonal communication"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0022-1031(04)00038-1","t":"Typicality and group variability as dual moderators of category-based inferences","a":["Alan J. Lambert"],"d":"2004-05-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0022-1031(04)00038-1","pdf":"","k":["Psychology","Social psychology","Extant taxon","Group (periodic table)","Homogeneous","Function (biology)","Contrast (vision)","Categorization"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2004.03.002","t":"Social value orientations and the strategic use of fairness in ultimatum bargaining","a":["Eric van Dijk","David De Cremer","Michel J. J. Handgraaf"],"d":"2004-04-14","abs":"","oa":"green","url":"https://research.tilburguniversity.edu/en/publications/a898e3e8-c050-4c7b-b2eb-6d719b9f6fd7","pdf":"https://research.tilburguniversity.edu/en/publications/a898e3e8-c050-4c7b-b2eb-6d719b9f6fd7","k":["Ultimatum game","Value (mathematics)","Psychology","Social psychology","Microeconomics","Economics"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2003.06.005","t":"On being sad and evasive: Affective influences on verbal communication strategies in conflict situations","a":["Joseph P. Forgas","Michelle Cromer"],"d":"2004-04-13","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2003.06.005","pdf":"","k":["Psychology","Affect (linguistics)","Mood","Situational ethics","Cognition","Social psychology","Nonverbal communication","Cognitive psychology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2004.02.002","t":"Perspective taking in children and adults: Equivalent egocentrism but differential correction","a":["Nicholas Epley","Carey K. Morewedge","Boaz Keysar"],"d":"2004-04-10","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2004.02.002","pdf":"","k":["Egocentrism","Psychology","Perspective (graphical)","Perspective-taking","Object (grammar)","Similarity (geometry)","Task (project management)","Developmental psychology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2004.01.004","t":"The man who wasn't there: Subliminal social comparison standards influence self-evaluation","a":["Thomas Mussweiler","Katja Rüter","Kai Epstude"],"d":"2004-04-10","abs":"Life provides an endless stream of social comparison information. Because opportunities to compare with others are so abundant, social comparison theory traditionally assumes that people are selective in their comparison activities and primarily compare with deliberately selected standards. Recent research, however, demonstrates that social comparisons often occur spontaneously, even if no standard is explicitly provided or deliberately selected. We examined whether comparisons are so spontaneous that they are even engaged if people are fleetingly exposed to a potential standard—so fleetingly that they remain unaware of the standard. In three studies, participants were subliminally primed with moderate versus extreme, high versus low standards during self-evaluation. Results demonstrate that self-evaluations are influenced by subliminally presented standards. Specifically, self-evaluations are assimilated towards moderate standards and contrasted away from extreme standards. These self-evaluative consequences of subliminal standards, however, were only obtained if participants engaged in self-reflection during standard exposure. These findings emphasize that social comparisons are truly ubiquitous processes that are engaged even for fleeting exposure to standard information.","oa":"hybrid","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022103104000228/pdf","pdf":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022103104000228/pdf","k":["Subliminal stimuli","Psychology","Social comparison theory","Social psychology","Self","Cognitive psychology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2004.03.001","t":"Self-activation is a two-edged sword: The effects of I primes on cooperation","a":["Sonja Utz"],"d":"2004-04-10","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2004.03.001","pdf":"","k":["Psychology","Social psychology","Priming (agriculture)","Prosocial behavior","Self construal","Cognition","Social dilemma","Social cognition"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2004.02.003","t":"Seeing is believing: Exposure to counterstereotypic women leaders and its effect on the malleability of automatic gender stereotyping","a":["Nilanjana Dasgupta","Shaki Asgari"],"d":"2004-04-09","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2004.02.003","pdf":"","k":["Psychology","Social psychology","Malleability","Ingroups and outgroups","Power (physics)","Field (mathematics)","Developmental psychology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2004.02.004","t":"Whose self is it anyway? Self-aspect control moderates the relation between self-complexity and well-being","a":["Allen R. McConnell","Jeanette M. Renaud","Kristy K. Dean","Sharin P. Green","Marika J. Lamoreaux","CARRIE E. HALL","Robert J. Rydell"],"d":"2004-04-09","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2004.02.004","pdf":"","k":["Psychology","Social psychology","Moderation","Relation (database)","Self","Self-concept","Self-control","Perception"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2004.02.001","t":"You've got mail: Using e-mail to examine the effect of prejudiced attitudes on discrimination against Arabs","a":["Brad J. Bushman","Angelica M. Bonacci"],"d":